# What line endings a file is stored with, decided here rather than by whichever machine last
# saved it.
#
# Four files in this repository were committed with CRLF for no reason anybody chose: two source
# files, the environment example, and a Windows batch script that genuinely wants them. The
# first three were invisible until somebody edited one — `core.autocrlf` is on for most people
# working on Windows, so any tool that rewrites a file normalises it on the way past, git then
# sees a different blob, and a fifteen-line change arrives as a four-hundred-line rewrite. That
# happened twice in one afternoon and was corrected by hand both times.
#
# `core.autocrlf` cannot fix it, because it is a setting on each person's own machine and this
# is a property of the repository. That is what this file is for: it applies to everybody who
# clones, whatever their git is configured to do, and it decides only what is *stored*. Working
# copies are unaffected — a Windows checkout still gets CRLF on disk if that is what the local
# config asks for.

# Everything git recognises as text is stored with LF. Binary content is detected and left
# alone, and the exceptions below are the files where the ending is load-bearing.
* text=auto

# ── Read by a shell ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# A shell script with CRLF endings fails on its own shebang: the interpreter path picks up the
# carriage return and the kernel reports "bad interpreter: /bin/sh^M", which is a confusing way
# to discover a line-ending problem at three in the morning. `docker-entrypoint.sh` is the one
# that would take a container down with it.
*.sh text eol=lf
apps/client/android/gradlew text eol=lf

# ── Read by cmd.exe ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# The mirror image, and the reason this file cannot simply say "LF everywhere". A batch file
# with LF endings parses unreliably on Windows — labels and `goto` in particular — so
# `gradlew.bat` keeps its CRLF, which it has had since Gradle generated it.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf

# ── Not text at all ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Detection by content would get these right anyway; naming them means a diff never tries to
# render one and no future setting can decide to "normalise" a PNG.
*.png  binary
*.jpg  binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif  binary
*.ico  binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf  binary
*.otf  binary
*.jar  binary
*.apk  binary
*.mp4  binary
*.webm binary
*.pdf  binary
*.zip  binary
